ch": "Turnout effects from the abortion and gender-transition ballot measure, and any late general-election polling, could shift the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Missouri remains a Republican-leaning state in presidential politics, and the 2028 open-seat race has drawn little general-election polling so far. The forecast rests on the state's partisan lean of about nine points Republican, with no incumbent on the ballot to shift the balance. Prediction markets have been skeptical of Democratic chances in the state, and headlines about Missouri politics center mostly on 2028 speculation around figures like Josh Hawley and Eric Schmitt rather than the presidential contest itself.\n\nThe main Democratic hope is the abortion-rights and gender-transition amendment on the 2026 ballot, which could lift turnout among Democratic-leaning voters and shape the electorate heading into 2028. Absent evidence that this changes the underlying landscape, the race stays in Likely R territory with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1545, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2074, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1871, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1883, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2013, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.183, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2061, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.204, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1925, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2006, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1989, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1976, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0